4fabb","89b7f5e29868cdd5","3624fdd666bdf17f"],"findings":"## Review of the structured-findings change\n\nI did not run anything. Every finding below is **read**, from the diff and the code it touches. The orchestrator's evidence (tsc clean, 424 tests passing) is consistent with these readings but is not something I re-checked.\n\n### 1. Mismatches\n\n**1a. `items-store` notes say the open count uses the newest review per part, but the code does not.** The notes read \"openItems counts the newest review per part of current stages.\" `openFindings` iterates every entry in `s.reviews` for each stage, and `latestReviews` is only used by the verdict path. The code counts every item of every structured review, including superseded ones. The README and tests agree with the code, so the annotation is what is wrong. **(read)**\n\n**1b. `table-and-gate` says `--open` checks \"current reviews\", but stale reviews count.** The notes say \"--open exits 1 on any undecided item of current reviews.\" `openFindings` sets `stale` as a label and keeps those items. The `--open` tests, and the orchestrator's passing run, show stale items still hold the gate shut. **(read; the passing test run is consistent with it)**\n\n**1c. `items-store` includes an unlabelled change.** Hunk h45 adds `adversarial` and `mutate` to `WORDS` in `src/reviews.ts`. That is a summary-wording change for two stages. It is not part of the block, the ids or the gate. The group's intent and notes never mention it, and its tests sit in `tests-reviews`. It is scope creep bundled into a `required` group. **(read)**\n\n**1d. Legacy decisions disappear from `show`.** `reviewsLine` no longer calls `decisionSummary`, so a legacy review's decisions no longer appear on the `show` line. The `remove-labels` and `docs` notes say old decisions \"stay readable and show as stored text\", which holds for `reviews` and the HTML pane but not `show`. The annotation does not record this. **(read)**\n\n**1e. Cosmetic: the table pads by UTF-16 length.** `table()` uses `String.length` for column widths, so CJK or emoji titles will misalign. This is not claimed anywhere. **(read)**\n\n### 2. Missing risks\n\n**2a. The spec's caps add up to more than the block cap.** `BLOCK_SPEC` promises up to 200 items with `detail` up to 2000 characters each. That is about 400 KB, but `MAX_BLOCK_BYTES` is 256 KiB. A reviewer that follows the spec for a large review gets a refusal (\"block is too long\") that the spec does not warn about. The `block-parser` note mentions the cap but not the mismatch with the spec. **(read)**\n\n**2b. Design deviation: order.** The approved design says `reviews` \"lists open ones first\". The implementation lists findings in reviewer order, which matches the author's own notes but not the approved design. Either the design or the code should change, and the author should say which. **(read)**\n\n**2c. The `--open` gate can stick.** A single unreadable record (for example a new `kind` enum value from a newer writer) keeps the gate shut for every reader until everyone upgrades. The author lists this. The stored data cannot be repaired by a decision, since the record is skipped entirely. This is the same concern as above, and I have no further evidence for it. **(read)**\n\n**2d. `show` and the HTML pane count only the commit's own decisions.** `reviews <target> --open` uses the union over the target, so a commit can show findings as open on `show` while the gate passes. The author's TODO records this. **(read)**\n\n### 3. Simplifications and removal candidates\n\nSkipped as requested, except where a claim is contradicted. The `checkLength` claim in the TODO is correct: note (2000 × 6 bytes) plus `by` (200 × 6) stays under 16 KiB. Nothing is contradicted there.\n\n### Summary of what is accurate\n\n- The block parser's fence rule, caps and `plain()` sanitising match the code.\n- `--record` refuses before any write and includes the spec in its message, as stated.\n- Decisions name item ids, and `--decide` and the template refuse legacy reviews, as stated.\n- `remove-labels` deletes the label machinery, and the `findings.ts` callers are gone.\n- Decisions are matched per review, so a decision cannot leak across reviews.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
