32cbd949a","9646d7237bece94c","ec0b0c7d07c5a488"],"findings":"## Review of the structured-findings change\n\nMost of the author's claims hold up against the code. The mismatches below are small and mostly in comments and docs. I did not grade necessity, per the brief.\n\n### 1. Mismatches\n\n- **Stale size comment (read).** `src/findings-block.ts:27` says 200 items of the longest strings \"come to about 450 KB\". That figure predates the detail cap drop from 2000 to 500. With the current caps the worst block is well under that, and the test \"the worst-case block the spec text allows fits\" asserts it fits. The comment is wrong, not the code.\n- **\"Every problem\" is capped (read).** The README and SKILL say `--record` refuses a report and prints \"every problem on stderr\". `checkItems` in `src/findings-block.ts` lists at most `MAX_LISTED = 25` problems, then \"... and N more problems\". The behaviour is reasonable, but the docs overstate it.\n- **`--open` says \"no open findings\" when nothing was checked (read).** On a target with no reviews, or only legacy reviews, `reviews <target> --open` prints \"no open findings\" and exits 0. That reads as a positive statement that the gate checked something. `hasItems` already exists for this distinction and is only used by `show`. The exit code is right; the message could say that no structured review was found.\n\n### 2. Missing risks\n\n- **Legacy is inferred from the absence of `items` (read).** A record with no `items` is treated as legacy: listed, but nothing to decide, and it hides nothing. Any writer that omits `items` escapes the gate for that record. Git notes are already trusted, so this does not add a new bypass. It does mean the gate can only be as strict as the writers are honest, and the docs should say so.\n- **Unbounded raw copy in `copyReviews` (read).** It copies non-decision note lines raw with no size bound. The TODO already records this as a trade-off, so it is acknowledged rather than missed.\n- **`skippedAt` can go stale (read).** `readReviews` returns early when `raw` is empty, so `skippedAt` keeps the previous count for that commit. The TODO records this too.\n\n### 3. Checked and holding (read unless noted)\n\n- **Verified:** `test/findings-block.test.ts` ran alone and passed 23/23. I did not re-run the full suite; the orchestrator's evidence reports 436 passing.\n- Dangling imports are clean: no remaining references to `findings.ts`, `labelFindings` or `orderDecisions` in `src`, `test` or `bin`.\n- The block spec is the same text in the review prompt, the README and the SKILL, and the test ties them together. The detail cap is 500 in code and docs.\n- Decisions are only read when their review is on the same commit (`readDecisions` checks `ids.has(r.review)`), so \"decisions only with a review on the commit\" holds per commit.\n- Malformed review lines are counted, and `--open` exits 1 on them. Over-cap decision lines are dropped silently, which fails closed for the gate, since an unread decision leaves its finding open.\n- The verdict-word addition for `adversarial` and `mutate` matches the code and the README.\n\n### 4. Removal candidates and simplifications\n\nI did not grade these, per the brief. The one concrete simplification is to reuse `hasItems` in `--open`'s \"no open findings\" branch, so the message can say when no structured review was found.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
